# Git Branching & Commits

When staging, committing, or pushing (including when the user asks for a commit):

## Never commit on master

- Do **not** commit on `master` or `main`.
- Before the first commit, create and switch to a new branch (see naming below).
- If currently on `master`/`main`, create the branch first, then stage and commit.

## Start from master

- **Always** check out `master` (or `main` if that is the default branch) **before** creating a new branch.
- Do not branch off another feature or bug branch unless the user explicitly asks.
- Pull latest on `master` when appropriate before branching.

## Branch naming

Use one of these formats with **today’s date** as `yyyymmdd` (e.g. `20260518`):

| Work type | Pattern | Example |
|-----------|---------|---------|
| Feature | `<feature>_yyyymmdd` | `employeeManagementApi_20260518` |
| Bug fix | `<bug>_yyyymmdd` | `duplicateEmailFix_20260518` |

- `<feature>` / `<bug>`: short camelCase or descriptive slug (no spaces).
- One branch per logical change; push with upstream when the user wants a remote branch (`git push -u origin <branch>`).

## Workflow

```bash
# 1. Switch to master and update if needed
git checkout master
git pull origin master

# 2. Create branch (feature or bug name + date)
git checkout -b myFeature_20260518

# 3. Stage, commit, push
git add ...
git commit -m "..."
git push -u origin myFeature_20260518
```

## Examples

```text
✅ employeeCrud_20260518          (feature)
✅ validationErrorResponse_20260518 (bug)

❌ commit on master
❌ git checkout -b myFeature_20260518   (from a feature branch — start from master first)
❌ feature/my-thing               (wrong format)
❌ myFeature_2026-05-18           (wrong date format)
```
